    id: "notes-on-standing-still",
    title: "Notes on Standing Still",
    author: "Marcus Whitfield",
    category: "Essay",
    text: `There is a particular kind of stillness available only on an escalator, and I don't think we talk about it enough. You are moving — genuinely, mechanically moving, carried at a fixed and mildly generous speed — while doing absolutely nothing yourself. No steps, no decisions, no effort. For thirty or ninety seconds, depending on the depth of the station, you are permitted to be transported without being responsible for the transportation.

I have come to think this is one of the few honest rests a city offers a person. Not a bench, which asks you to have somewhere to be later and time to spare now, a trade most commuters can't make. Not a coffee break, which has become its own small performance of productivity — even rest, these days, wants a receipt. The escalator asks nothing. It doesn't care if you stand on the left, contrary to the signage and the tutting; it will still deliver you.

I started paying attention to this during a bad year, the kind where the smallest unstructured moment felt like a held breath. What I noticed was how differently people occupy an escalator once they stop treating it as an obstacle to be overtaken. Some close their eyes. Some read the adverts with more attention than the adverts have earned, working out what specific loneliness is being sold to them this month. A woman near Oxford Circus once spent the whole ride redoing her ponytail with the unhurried precision of someone at a mirror at home, not a stranger in transit surrounded by other strangers.

There's a school of thought — productivity culture's most tireless export — that says any unclaimed minute is a minute wasted, that the ride down to the Victoria line is an opportunity to answer three emails or listen to a podcast at double speed so you can call it "caught up." I understand the impulse. I have succumbed to it, thumb twitching toward a notification before the top step has even flattened out beneath my feet.

But I've started resisting it, in small deliberate ways, because I think the escalator is quietly teaching us something about rest that we've otherwise forgotten: that it doesn't have to be earned, scheduled, or productive to count. It just has to be permitted to happen while something else — gravity, a chain drive, the ordinary machinery of the city — does the moving for you.

Underground, standing still while in motion is not a metaphor I'm reaching for; it's a literal daily event available to millions of people, most of whom spend it checking their phones. I don't say this with judgement. I say it as an invitation, the kind a stranger might extend without expecting it to be taken: next time, try just standing. Let the tunnel walls slide by tiled and lit and slightly blurred. Let the thirty seconds be thirty seconds, unmonetised, unoptimised, entirely and only themselves.

You'll arrive at the bottom either way. That was never in question. The only question is what you were doing on the way down.`,
